  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="FR">Output 2.1. Increased capacity of the member States to ratify international labour standards</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="FR">The Occupational Safety and Health Convention, 1981 (No. 155) was ratified and registered on the 1st of October 2021. The assistance provided to Cameroon in order to ease the ratification of Convention No. 155, preceded the current biennium. 
The initial confusion as to the effective ratification of Convention No. 155 was originally addressed in June 2018 during a normative activity held in Douala (&#xab; Atelier de vulgarisation des normes internationales du travail (NIT) ratifi&#xe9;es par le Cameroun et sensibilisation sur les maladies professionnelles et les accidents de travail. &#xbb; The follow-up provided during the current biennium was done in the context of the Recommendations of the Standards Review Mechanism Tripartite Working Group (SRM TWG) and the Centenary Ratification Campaign.

By communication dated 3 April 2019, the Head of the Labour Standards and International Cooperation Division of the Ministry of Labour and Social Security indicated that the likelihood of ratification of the Occupational Safety and Health Convention, 1981 (No. 155) was high. She added that the Government would resume/re-launch the processes for the Social Security (Minimum Standards) Convention, 1952 (No. 102); the Human Resources Development Convention, 1975 (No. 142); and the Domestic Workers Convention, 2011 (No. 189). 
The ratification decree was signed by the President of the Republic on 29 June 2020 (but the instrument was not deposited until the 1st of October 2021).</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="FR">2.1.2. Number of ratifications of up-to-date technical Conventions, including those recommended by the Governing Body in the context of the Standards Review Mechanism</narrative>
    </title>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="FR">Output 2.3. Increased capacity of the ILO constituents to engage in a forward-looking international labour standards policy</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="FR">The Office received all 9 article 22 reports due within the deadline. They are on Conventions No. 29, 87, 94, 105, 122, 138, 144, 158 and 182.

 To enable member states on the continent to make up for the delays of two years or more, the Office decided to prioritise actions aimed at providing technical support to the countries most in difficulty (&quot;serious cases of non-compliance&quot; and &quot;emergency appeals&quot;). In this case, although Cameroon is not among those most in difficulty, the support took the form of one (01) scholarship to participate in the online training course entitled &quot;Best practices on the preparation of reports on international labour standards&quot; (from 10 May to 2 July 2021).  

During the current biennium (2020-2021), the Heads of the Division of Standards and International Labour Cooperation and the International Labour Standards Unit within the Ministry of Labour and Social Security (MINTSS) have been trained in international labour standards in 2020 and 2021, respectively. Moreover, the head of the Unit, who benefited from the abovementioned scholarship,  chaired the Committee on the Application of Standards (C. App.) of the International Labour Conference (105th Session, Geneva, May-June 2021).

In addition, two tripartite activities were carried out to assist the member State in meeting its 2021 reporting obligations, on the 17th of May and 19th of August 2021, in response to a request by the MINTSS (&quot;R&#xe9;union Consultative en vue de l&apos;&#xe9;labortion des rapports des conventions de l&apos;OIT ratifi&#xe9;es par le Cameroun&quot;).</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="FR">2.3.1. Percentage of reports on the application of ratified Conventions due by 1 September received in a timely manner that include replies to comments of the supervisory bodies</narrative>
    </title>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
